  @edge-case @security
  Scenario: A chat request whose payload cannot be decoded as a command envelope is dropped without crashing the gateway
    Given a message arrives on the Jarvis command subject whose bytes are not a valid command envelope
    When the chat gateway tries to decode the message
    Then the chat gateway should record the decode failure
    And the chat gateway should not invoke the supervisor for that message
    And the chat gateway should remain subscribed and able to handle further requests

  # ─── Concurrency ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  # Why: Single-session-per-gateway has a real semantic cost when two requesters arrive at once
  # [ASSUMPTION: confidence=medium] In Phase 1 the gateway runs a single shared chat session; concurrent chats are serialised through that session so the supervisor never sees overlapping turns, accepting the trade-off that two unrelated requesters can observe each other's context until per-requester sessions land in a later phase
  @edge-case @concurrency
  Scenario: Two concurrent chats on the single shared session are answered one turn at a time
    Given two chat requests arrive on the Jarvis command subject before either has been answered
    When the chat gateway handles both requests
    Then the supervisor should answer them one turn at a time on the single shared session
    And each reply should carry the correlation identifier of its own request
    And the second turn should be able to see the context produced by the first turn

  # Why: Shutdown ordering — heartbeat must lose to deregister so the broker's last write is the goodbye
  @edge-case @concurrency
  Scenario: A shutdown that arrives mid-heartbeat lets the deregister be the last fleet write
    Given the heartbeat loop is about to publish a fleet heartbeat when the operator interrupts the chat gateway
    When the chat gateway shuts down
    Then the heartbeat loop should be stopped before Jarvis deregisters from the fleet
    And the deregister write should be the last fleet observation the broker records for Jarvis

  # ─── Data integrity ──────────────────────────────────────────────────

  # Why: Bug #1 under the realistic failure where the requester walked away — observers must still see the reply
  @edge-case @regression
  Scenario: A reply produced after the requester has timed out still lands on the canonical result topic
    Given Rich sends a chat request and stops waiting before the supervisor finishes
